<LegalPage
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How Vector handles account information, local work, AI requests, connected services, diagnostics, and usage counts."
  updated="October 10, 2026"
>
  <section>
    <h2>1. Scope and contact</h2>
    <p>
      This Policy explains information handling for the Vector website, desktop application, command-line
      tools, and Vector-operated account, support, and cloud endpoints. “Vector,” “we,” and “us” refer
      to Krishna B / VectorDev.ai, the operator of those services. For privacy questions or requests, email
      <a href="mailto:krishnabharadwaj0521@gmail.com">krishnabharadwaj0521@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vector keeps much of your work on the computer running the application. AI requests, connected tools,
      help, and publishing involve other systems. The data sent depends on your instructions, selected
      model, enabled features, permissions, and connected accounts. Independent providers also process
      information under their own terms and privacy policies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Account and sign-in information</h2>
    <p>
      Supabase provides account authentication and stores your email address, account and authentication
      identifiers, sign-in provider, and profile information supplied at sign-in, such as your name. If you use
      Google sign-in, Google processes that authentication and supplies the permitted account information.
      We use account information to authenticate you, verify access, and respond to account requests. CLI access may use an account-linked token for verification.
    </p>
    <p>
      Website sign-in does not itself upload your repository or local conversation history. Authentication
      uses browser storage to keep your session signed in and complete sign-in redirects. Signing out or
      clearing that storage removes the local session but does not delete the account.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Local projects, conversations, and memory</h2>
    <p>
      Repositories, session history, tool results, preferences, checkpoints, workspace metadata, and local
      logs are generally stored on the computer running Vector. The embedded browser can retain cookies,
      sign-in sessions, and other website storage on that computer. Connected remote runtimes or servers may
      instead hold the data they process.
    </p>
    <p>
      Optional local memory stores notes and preferences in a local configuration file. You can inspect and
      erase that file in Settings. Relevant memory and instructions can be included in requests to your
      selected model. Deleting local memory does not delete a copy already sent to a provider, copied into
      conversation history, or included in a backup.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Dictation.</strong> With microphone permission, desktop dictation processes recorded audio on
      your computer using a local Whisper model. The model is downloaded and cached; its hosting service
      receives the download request, but this transcription path does not upload your audio. Browser dictation
      uses the browser's speech-recognition service, which may send audio to a remote provider under that
      browser or provider's privacy practices. The resulting text enters the composer and, when submitted,
      is handled like a typed prompt. Stop recording or revoke microphone permission to prevent further capture.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Models and AI request content</h2>
    <p>
      To answer a task, Vector may send prompts, conversation history, selected or agent-read files, code,
      images, instructions, memory, repository metadata, and tool output to the selected model service.
      Tool output can include terminal text, search results, browser content, or information returned by an
      integration. Content is selected according to the task and enabled permissions; it may include personal
      or confidential information present in those sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      Model requests use the provider you connect and its service and account. Vector no longer uses
      the former third-party model gateway. Requests can pass through a configured gateway
      or proxy. Each recipient's retention, abuse-review, and model-training rules depend on the service and
      your agreement or settings with it. Vector does not promise that every provider offers zero retention
      or excludes all request content from training.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. External agents, plugins, and connected tools</h2>
    <p>
      If you select an external runtime such as Claude Code, Codex CLI, or Cursor Agent, Vector can pass your
      task and working directory to the installed program and display its responses. That program may read
      project files, use its own credentials, transmit context, and maintain its own logs and history under
      its own permissions and provider policies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Plugins, MCP servers, repository services, and other integrations receive the requests and data needed
      for the actions you authorize. Responses can enter the local conversation and subsequent model requests.
      A standing permission or scheduled task may allow multiple operations without a new prompt each time.
      Disconnecting an integration prevents future use through that connection but does not remove records
      the provider already received or revoke credentials everywhere they may be used.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Live workspace invitations.</strong> A live workspace invitation contains guest credentials.
      People who have the link and can reach your server can access the workspace through the guest account;
      a tunnel provider may also process connection traffic. Share invitations only with trusted people and
      stop the server to end live access.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6. Browser and computer use</h2>
    <p>
      Browser automation can inspect page text, URLs, page structure, screenshots, and technical browser
      output, and interact with sites you open. Those sites receive ordinary browser traffic and any
      information submitted to them; their cookies and privacy policies apply.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where available and enabled, computer-use features or optional plugins can list available targets,
      capture a selected window or screen, and send mouse or keyboard input. These features process window
      or application names, target and frame identifiers, screenshots, and requested input actions.
      Operating-system permissions, including screen recording and accessibility permissions on macOS, may
      also be required. Availability and controls depend on your platform, application version, and plugin.
    </p>
    <p>
      Screenshots and action results can be stored as session content and sent to your selected model provider.
      They may reveal notifications, messages, personal information, or confidential content visible in the
      selected area. Close or hide unrelated sensitive content before use. You can stop the session, disable
      the feature or plugin, and revoke operating-system permissions. Stopping future capture does not delete
      previously recorded session content or copies already received by a provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>7. Cloud connections and published content</h2>
    <p>
      Cloud features can transmit project files, build output, deployment settings, database operations,
      resource identifiers, and configuration to providers such as Vercel, Netlify, Supabase, AWS, GitHub, or
      GitLab, depending on the connection and action. Responses, resource metadata, and logs may be displayed
      or retained locally and included in agent context when you use them in a task.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some connections use Vector-operated OAuth endpoints to exchange authorization codes, refresh access,
      or revoke a connection. Those endpoints process the necessary codes, access or refresh tokens, and
      transaction metadata before returning the result to the application. Credentials are therefore not
      confined to your device throughout every connection flow.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you publish through a Vector-hosted publishing feature, the selected files and deployment metadata
      are uploaded to the hosting infrastructure; public site files may be stored in Vercel Blob and served
      to anyone with access to their URLs. Publishing can disclose content publicly. Remove private data and
      secrets before uploading. Local deletion, sign-out, or account deletion does not itself remove
      a hosted deployment; use the hosting provider's controls or contact support about Vector-hosted content.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Vector-hosted public sessions.</strong> Where this optional service is available, publishing a
      session stores its visible transcript in Vector's Supabase service and makes it readable by anyone with
      the link, without signing in. The transcript includes its title, visible prompts, assistant responses,
      displayed reasoning, code, and tool input and output. Review it before publishing: these fields may
      contain personal information, confidential material, or secrets, and Vector does not guarantee automatic
      removal of sensitive content. Account credentials, application settings, and hidden system instructions
      are excluded as separate data fields, but credentials or other sensitive information appearing in the
      visible text can still be published. Attachments are represented by descriptive labels; the public viewer
      does not fetch the attached files or their URLs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public sharing requires explicit consent. You choose whether future updates to that conversation are
      public and select an expiry no later than 30 days after publication. If you separately choose to remember
      account-wide consent for automatic sharing, future sessions can be published when automatic sharing is
      enabled, including sensitive text they may contain. Project automatic-share settings alone do not
      authorize uploads. Disable automatic sharing to stop new automatic publications and use Unshare to
      remove existing public copies. Sharing is optional and is not required to use Vector.
    </p>
    <p>
      Successful unsharing, link expiry, account-access revocation, or account deletion stops public access
      through Vector. Successful unsharing and account deletion erase the live transcript; expired live
      transcripts are erased by scheduled cleanup. If a removal request fails, the link may remain public;
      retry it or contact support. Deleted or expired content may remain in service-provider backups until
      those backups expire under the configured retention policy. Contact us for the applicable storage
      location and backup-retention information. People who have already viewed a transcript may keep their
      own copies, which Vector cannot erase. The viewer asks search engines not to index the page, but this
      does not make a public link private or prevent others from sharing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      To prevent delayed upload requests from restoring removed links, we retain a minimal deletion record
      indefinitely without the transcript. It contains the random share identifier, a hash of its management
      secret, and lifecycle metadata, linked to your account identifier until account deletion clears that
      link. These records are not returned by the public viewer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Earlier third-party shares.</strong> The former third-party public-share service is no longer
      used for new publication in Vector. This does not remove copies published there by earlier releases.
      Removing a Vector-hosted public session, deleting local history, or deleting your Vector account does
      not remove those historical copies. Their removal requires the former service's controls or separate
      cleanup, and recipients may also retain copies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>8. Help and support</h2>
    <p>
      The in-app help assistant sends your recent help conversation and matching Vector documentation through
      a Vector-operated endpoint to Groq to generate an answer. It does not automatically attach your
      repository or coding-session history. Anything you type or paste into the help conversation is part of
      the information sent. Service error logs may include upstream error details.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you submit a bug report, we receive the description, any optional reply email, application version,
      operating system, architecture, release channel, and technical context included by that report flow.
      Resend delivers the report to our support mailbox. Email you send directly also becomes support
      correspondence. Avoid including credentials or unnecessary confidential information.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>9. Diagnostics and service logs</h2>
    <p>
      Remote crash diagnostics are off by default. If you enable them and reporting is configured, Sentry
      receives application version, platform, stack traces, and technical error information. Vector disables
      default personally identifiable information and performance tracing and removes request, user,
      breadcrumb, and extra fields from diagnostic events before sending them. Error text and stack traces
      can still contain incidental information. Repository files, prompts, terminal output, and credentials
      are not intentionally attached. You can disable future reporting in Settings. Local crash dumps and
      application logs remain on your computer unless you separately share them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our website, API, hosting, and security providers process network information such as IP addresses,
      request time, browser or client details, requested routes, and response or error information to deliver
      and protect the service. These operational records are separate from optional desktop diagnostics.
      Update and download requests also disclose ordinary connection and requested-release information.
    </p>
    <p>
      Help and bug-report abuse controls use keyed identifiers derived from network addresses and, for some
      reports, a derived email identifier. These identifiers are pseudonymous, not necessarily anonymous.
      The help counter expires after ten minutes; bug-report network counters expire after one hour and
      email counters after 24 hours. Expiration of those counters does not delete separate provider logs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>10. Usage counts and website analytics</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>What is counted.</strong> To learn how many people use Vector, whether they come back, which
      versions and platforms to support, and how much model use runs through Vector, the desktop application
      and the command-line tool report usage counts to Vector. The desktop application sends a random install
      identifier created on your computer, your account identifier when you are signed in, the application
      version, the operating system and CPU architecture, and the number of sessions and subagent sessions
      active that day (in UTC). It reports shortly after it opens each day and again, checked about once an
      hour, whenever something it reports has gone up. The command-line tool's daily account check, which
      already identifies your account, also reports the tool's version, operating system and CPU architecture
      at the first command of each UTC day, and counts that day as a day of command-line use.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Model use.</strong> With those reports, the desktop application, and the command-line tool once
      each UTC day, also send the totals shown in the desktop application's Usage &amp; streaks settings, worked
      out on your computer: token totals by type (input, output, reasoning, and cached) and the number of model
      responses; the model cost the application recorded; tokens, tasks, and recorded cost for each of the last
      seven days with any use; the models and providers you use most (up to five), as their identifiers with
      each one's tokens and share of your tokens; the reasoning effort levels used, with their tokens, responses,
      and share; the number of chats completed and conversations; your active days and current and longest
      streaks; and your average and longest task time. The command-line tool sends these with a random install
      identifier that Vector does not store, because its counts are kept against your account.
    </p>
    <p>
      Usage counts never include prompts, code, file names or paths, model output, provider keys or
      credentials, IP addresses, or user agents. They are stored in Vector's Supabase service as one record per
      install or account per day, with the latest of these totals and the tokens, tasks, and cost of each day.
      Vector's usage dashboard shows only aggregate totals, for example how many installs were active in a week
      or how many tokens were processed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Aggregated statistics.</strong> Vector may share aggregated, non-identifying statistics drawn from
      usage counts, such as the number of active users, tokens processed, or popular models, with investors,
      partners, and the public, including through read-only links to its usage dashboard. These are totals
      across everyone and never include an account, install identifier, or email address. Models and custom
      effort levels that fewer than three people use are left out of anything shared this way.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Downloads.</strong> Installer downloads require a Vector account. Each download is recorded
      against the signed-in account with the installer platform, the version, and the time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Retention and deletion.</strong> Usage counts, including the model-use totals, and download
      records are deleted automatically after about 13 months. Deleting your Vector account deletes the usage
      counts and download records linked to it, including earlier days reported by an install before it was
      linked to your account. Aggregated statistics already shared, which identify no one, are not withdrawn.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Turning it off.</strong> Usage counts are on by default. In the desktop application, turn off
      Settings → General → “Share usage counts” to stop check-ins, including the model-use totals. For the
      command-line tool, set the environment variable <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_USAGE=1</code>; it then sends no
      model-use totals, and its daily account check still runs, because sign-in depends on it, but asks Vector
      not to count it. Versions released before usage counts existed do not report them. To limit abuse, the check-in service keeps a keyed counter derived from the network
      address for one hour; it is separate from the usage counts and is not stored with them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Website visits.</strong> Visits to public pages of the website and documentation are counted with
      Vercel Web Analytics, a cookie-free service that reports aggregate figures such as page views, visitors,
      referring sites, countries, browsers, and device types. It sets no cookies, and the address sent is
      reduced to the page path, without query strings. Account, sign-in, private, and shared-session pages are
      not counted. Vercel, which hosts the website, processes the request itself, including its network
      address, to produce those figures.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>11. Why information is used and who receives it</h2>
    <p>
      We use information to provide requested features, authenticate accounts, deliver communications, answer support requests, troubleshoot failures, prevent fraud and
      abuse, secure the service, understand how much Vector is used through the aggregate counts in section 10,
      and meet legal obligations. Aggregated statistics that identify no one, described in section 10, may also
      be shared with investors, partners, and the public. Recipients include the account, email,
      hosting, security, and diagnostic providers described above and the model and integration providers
      involved in your selected workflow.
    </p>
    <p>
      Information may also be disclosed when necessary to comply with a lawful obligation, establish or defend
      legal claims, or protect people and service security. If the business is reorganized or transferred,
      relevant account and service records may transfer with it, subject to applicable law and appropriate
      notice. This Policy does not authorize unrelated use of your project content.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where a law requires a legal basis for processing, the applicable basis depends on the purpose: providing
      a service you request; legitimate interests in security, abuse prevention,
      service support, and measuring use through usage counts, which you can turn off, balanced against your rights; compliance with legal obligations; or consent for
      optional processing such as public session sharing and remote crash diagnostics. You can withdraw consent
      for optional processing without affecting processing that was lawful before withdrawal. Required account information
      is necessary to provide those features; optional diagnostics are not a condition of using Vector.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>12. Storage, security, and retention</h2>
    <p>
      Packaged desktop builds use the operating system's secure storage for supported provider credentials
      and the local credential-vault key. Some application data, including local memory,
      uses local files instead. External runtimes have their own storage practices. Device access controls,
      backups, and the permissions you grant also affect security. No storage or transmission method can
      guarantee absolute security.
    </p>
    <p>
      Local data remains until removed through application controls or from the relevant files, subject to
      local cleanup and backups. Account records are retained while needed to provide access
      and resolve account issues. Records of earlier purchases are retained as required by law, including for
      tax, accounting, fraud, and dispute obligations. Payment records of those purchases are held by our
      payment processor, Stripe, and kept only as the law requires. Support records and diagnostic or operational logs are retained according to
      their troubleshooting and security purpose and applicable provider settings. Usage counts and download
      records are kept for about 13 months. We consider the type of
      record, whether the account or issue is active, legal retention requirements, and unresolved disputes
      when deciding retention. No single deletion period applies to every category.
    </p>
    <p>
      Providers may process information outside your country, with different privacy laws. Locations and
      transfer arrangements depend on the provider and service configuration. Contact us for information
      about the arrangements relevant to your account. A provider's independent retention rules continue
      to apply to information already sent to it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>13. Your choices and privacy rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can choose models and integrations, limit permissions, stop background work or computer use,
      disconnect providers, turn off diagnostics, usage counts (<strong>Share usage counts</strong> in the desktop
      application, or <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_USAGE=1</code> for the command-line tool), and automatic sharing,
      unshare public sessions, edit or erase
      local memory, and remove local sessions and other data. Removing a local session alone does not remove
      its public copy. Use the relevant provider's controls to revoke its credentials or manage records it holds.
      Deleting an account, deleting local data, and deleting provider-held content are
      separate actions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Depending on the law that applies to you, you may have rights to access or receive a copy of personal
      information, correct it, request deletion, object to or restrict processing, withdraw consent, or appeal
      a request decision. You may also have rights relating to sale, sharing, or targeted advertising where
      those activities occur. These rights can have exceptions, including necessary security and legal
      records. We will not unlawfully discriminate against you for exercising an applicable right.
    </p>
    <p>
      Send requests to <a href="mailto:krishnabharadwaj0521@gmail.com">krishnabharadwaj0521@gmail.com</a>.
      Identify the account and the request without sending passwords, full card numbers, or private keys.
      We may need proportionate identity or authorized-agent verification before responding. We will respond
      within the period required by applicable law and explain a refusal where required. You can also complain
      to the privacy regulator where you live or work, including the UK Information Commissioner's Office
      where applicable. We cannot directly erase data held independently by a provider you connected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>14. Children and policy updates</h2>
    <p>
      Vector is intended for adults and is not directed to children. If you believe a child has supplied
      personal information to our account or service endpoints, contact us so we can review and address it.
    </p>
    <p>
      We update this Policy when relevant practices change and show the effective date above. Material changes
      will be communicated through the service or an account contact where appropriate. We will provide any
      additional notice or obtain consent required by law before using information for a materially different
      purpose.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalPage>
